conspy: KDGKBMODE returns an int

conspy reads the keyboard mode into a long. KDGKBMODE writes an int, so on
64-bit the upper half is whatever was on the stack. When it is not zero,
the K_XLATE/K_UNICODE check fails, conspy takes the console for scan code
mode and drops every key it was given. Seen on arm64 (the Nexus 5X) when
conspy runs over SSH; from telnet the stack happened to be zero.

--- a/miscutils/conspy.c
+++ b/miscutils/conspy.c
@@ -533,7 +533,7 @@
 		G.key_count += bytes_read;
 		if (G.escape_count == 0) {
 			int handle, result;
-			long kbd_mode;
+			int kbd_mode;
 
 			handle = xopen(tty_name, O_WRONLY);
 			result = ioctl(handle, KDGKBMODE, &kbd_mode);
